---
/**
 * The deferred "run your own benchmark" page.
 *
 * Emitted only when RUN_YOUR_OWN_ENABLED is true at build time. When the flag
 * is off, getStaticPaths returns nothing and the page is absent from dist/ —
 * not hidden with CSS, not returning a 404 page, simply not built.
 *
 * It is a **rest route** (`[...path]`) rather than a plain `run.astro` for one
 * specific reason: Astro only calls getStaticPaths for dynamic routes. A static
 * page ignores it entirely and is emitted unconditionally, which is exactly the
 * bug the build test caught when this was `run.astro`.
 *
 * Until the proxy is deployed this is a stub that says what is coming and,
 * more importantly, exactly what will and will not happen to a visitor's API
 * keys. Making those commitments before building the thing is the point: they
 * constrain the implementation rather than describing it after the fact.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import { REPO_URL, RUN_YOUR_OWN_ENABLED, routes } from '../../lib/urls.ts'

export function getStaticPaths() {
  // An empty list means Astro emits no page at all for this route.
  return RUN_YOUR_OWN_ENABLED ? [{ params: { path: undefined } }] : []
}
---

<Base
  title="Run your own"
  description="Run the Sandwich Classification Benchmark on your own question, with your own provider keys."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Run your own benchmark</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A planned capability: sign in, supply your own provider keys, ask your own question, and get a
      full report rendered in your browser.
    </p>

    <div class="callout status">
      <p>
        <strong>Not yet available.</strong> This page describes what is being built. The control below
        is disabled because the service behind it does not exist yet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What it will do</h2>

    <p>
      Sign in with an En Dash account, enter API keys for the providers you want to evaluate, type a
      research question, choose models, and run. Results render with the same report components as
      the published editions — masthead, KPI tiles, quadrant, leaderboard, vendor profiles — under a
      persistent banner marking the run as unofficial.
    </p>

    <p>
      The runner is the same code that produces the weekly editions. That is already true and
      already tested: a CI test bundles the runner core and every adapter for a browser target with
      no Node polyfills and executes a benchmark in headless Chromium.
    </p>

    <h2>What will happen to your keys</h2>

    <p>These are commitments, published before the feature exists so that they constrain it:</p>

    <ul class="commitments">
      <li>
        <strong>Held in browser session storage only.</strong> Never in a cookie, never in local storage,
        never persisted past the tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cleared on sign-out</strong> and when the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sent only to the proxy origin</strong>, over TLS, once per request, and never to any
        other host.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never stored server-side and never logged.</strong> The proxy records a user id, a provider,
        a status and a duration. Not headers, not keys, not prompts, not bodies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never written to <code>data/</code></strong>. Your runs are yours; they are not
        published and do not enter the archive.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The proxy exists because a static site cannot call a provider API directly without exposing
      keys to every origin the browser talks to, and because a benchmark hosted on GitHub Pages has
      nowhere to put a secret. Its threat model will be published at
      <code>docs/proxy.md</code> alongside the implementation.
    </p>

    <h2>Available now: run it locally</h2>

    <p>
      Everything above already works from the command line, where your keys never leave your
      machine:
    </p>

    <pre><code>git clone {REPO_URL}.git
cd hotdogbenchmark && npm install
cp .env.example .env      # add your keys
npm run bench -- run</code></pre>

    <p>
      Edit <code>questions.json</code> to ask something else. The
      <a href={routes.howItWorks()}>how it works</a> page explains the pipeline, and
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/self-hosting.md`}>self-hosting</a> covers running it weekly
      in your own fork.
    </p>

    <p>
      <button type="button" disabled aria-describedby="signin-note"> Sign in with En Dash </button>
      <span id="signin-note" class="muted">Not yet available.</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .callout {
    padding: var(--space-m);
    background: var(--surface);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    margin-block: var(--space-l);
  }

  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .commitments li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  button[disabled] {
    font: inherit;
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-s);
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    color: var(--muted);
    border: var(--hairline) solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    cursor: not-allowed;
  }
</style>
